<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>KhaozEngine.Catalog.GameTypes</PackageId>
    <Version>$(KhaozEngineVersion)</Version>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <Description>The thirteen game-shaped content types most RPG-shaped worlds re-author from scratch (in Foundation, over KhaozEngine.Catalog): food, equip_profile and its stat lines, store and its shelves, monster_drop, gathering_node, recipe with its inputs and outputs, tool_tier, skill_curve and game_tuning. Each one is a stable type id in the game band, a stable key, an ordered field schema, a row codec and one Register call that supplies all of them plus the type's visibility and chunk slots, so two worlds that author the same facts store them the same way and address them the same way. A GAME DECLARES ITS TIME UNIT through ContentDurationUnit and the four duration fields take the matching name, whole ticks as an Int or hundredths of a second as a ScaledInt at scale 100, with identical field order and row bytes under either unit. ELEVEN TYPES SHIP A VALIDATOR, each a nested Validator on its type class emitting the KGT finding codes, and GameContentTypes.Register puts all thirteen on a registry in one call from one GameContentOptions whose members are all required, so a game cannot forget a seam. equip_profile and game_tuning ship none on purpose. The two GAME SEAMS are predicates over the raw stored number and never an enum or a roster: RecipeValidatorOptions carries a known repeat mode, a payable skill, an open skill and a nameable station, with two separate skill predicates so KGT0702 and KGT0703 stay distinct, and SkillCurveContentType.Validator takes one known-skill predicate and prints the raw number. StoreContentType carries BasisPointDenominator and LargestSafeRateBasisPoints, and its validator reads the engine item type's value field position off the LIVE registry at validation time rather than off a schema built at type load. Every rule is unit-neutral, so one validator serves Ticks and Seconds. THE CROSS-TYPE SWEEP IS HERE: GameContentChecks holds the sixteen rules no single type can state, codes KGT1301 to KGT1307 and KGT1309 to KGT1317 with the seven loot number rules and the drops-per-kill maximum among them, and GameContentSweepOptions names the max-level knob, the max-chance knob, the knob list and the drops-per-kill knob they read, with a null name disabling that rule and an empty list disabling the required-knob rule. It rides ONE registration slot, the lowest game id, composed over that type's own validator, because the engine hands every per-type validator the whole candidate and thirteen mountings would report every defect thirteen times. A game adds its own whole-catalog rules through GameContentSweepOptions.GameRules, engine IContentValidators that run in that slot after the package's own, over the same candidate and into the same findings. The package owns no vocabulary of its own: a skill, a station, a repeat mode, an npc kind and a monster kind are all raw numbers a game gives meaning to, and there is no enum, no balance number and no roster anywhere in it. Pure .NET, no JSON, no external deps.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The positional *Index constants are internal on purpose: a position is not a consumer's to write
         down. The test project reaches them to pin each one against its schema's own IndexOf, which is what
         makes an off-by-one a failing test rather than a read of the neighbouring field. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="KhaozEngine.Catalog.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../KhaozEngine.Catalog/KhaozEngine.Catalog.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
